The database looked healthy.

The two messages about money never reached a person.

Both emails had been read. Both had been classified. Both had clean records in the table.

The final alert had failed.

I recognised the shape of the problem before I knew the cause.

## A queue is only honest when attention reaches the right place

Early in my career, I worked in technical support and later handled real-time queue operations on a busy support floor.

The work was less glamorous than the dashboard made it look. A queue might appear stable while one difficult case sat too long. A break plan might look efficient while the floor lost coverage at the wrong minute.

Numbers describe movement. They do not prove the right person saw the right problem.

The same tension returned when I built an Inbox Triage Agent for my own operations.

I wanted fewer interruptions, but I did not want money, legal language or urgent work buried inside a quiet inbox.

## The build had a simple promise

The workflow reads an inbound email, decides what kind of message it is and pulls out useful details.

For a quote request, it looks for the service, quantity, deadline and budget. Missing information stays missing. The system does not invent a number to make the record look complete.

Spam is logged without an alert.

Refunds, chargebacks, overdue invoices, disputes and legal language go to a person. So does high urgency. A draft with an unresolved price field also goes to a person.

The workflow drafts replies but never sends them.

## The worst bug looked like success

Two test messages entered the database correctly. One was a quote request. The other chased an overdue invoice and mentioned a lawyer.

Telegram rejected both alerts.

The reason was small: the category names contained underscores. Telegram read them as formatting marks and rejected the message.

The categories carrying the most risk were the categories no one saw.

I changed the alert format and created safe copies of every field before sending it to Telegram. The original email stayed untouched in storage.

A later test included angle brackets and an ampersand in the subject. The alert arrived.

## Four runs measured more than speed

Four live executions averaged 3.19 seconds.

One quote request described 40 orders per day and a deadline before the Christmas rush. The workflow found both details and left the budget blank because the sender never gave one.

A spam message entered the log without disturbing anyone.

Then I installed the repository from scratch and tested the full path against a clean instance. A real Telegram message ID became the proof of delivery.

Green boxes were no longer enough.

## I built the queue I used to watch by hand

My real-time operations work taught me to care about the space between a recorded event and human attention.

The Inbox Triage Agent exists for the same reason.

It does not replace judgment. It protects judgment from noise, then brings the serious message forward with the details already organised.

A quiet inbox is useful only when silence means nothing important was missed.

Built by handlit.
